{-
   What a function **value** refuses - Analysis-IR.md §11.4's G7.

   Three, and all three are about the language rather than about the milestone: each is a shape
   there is no right answer for, and the messages say which. What a *lambda* refuses is a different
   list and is in `Reject.Closure.yana`, because those are questions about where an environment
   lives and none of them is a question about a function value.

   `Callable.yana` is the other side of the same feature - a callable *requirement*, discharged
   where a call is written - and nothing here overlaps it: a requirement never becomes a value.
-}

fn twice(x: I32) -> I32 = x + x

fn twice(x: I32, y: I32) -> I32 = x + y

fn take(f: (I32) -> I32) -> I32 = f(1 :: I32)

fn (a) polymorphic(->x: a) -> a = x

{-
   A name with **no position to pick its arity from**.

   "One plain function per name, any arity" makes the number of arguments part of what a use
   selects, and a call says it by writing them. A value has only the type it is being bound to, so a
   position that expects nothing has nothing to select with - and there are two `twice`s here to
   make that a real ambiguity rather than a rule with one candidate.
-}
fn unpositioned() -> I32:
    let f = twice
    return 0 :: I32

-- The right name at the right **arity** and the wrong shape. Reported against the whole value
-- rather than against one position, because a value has to accept every call the position will
-- make - so what the message names is the two types and not the argument that disagreed.
fn narrow(x: I64) -> I32 = 1 :: I32

fn mismatched() -> I32 = take(narrow)

-- A **generic** function has no single body for a value to point at: `polymorphic` is a family, and
-- which member of it the pair would hold is a question the pair has nowhere to keep the answer to.
fn generic() -> I32 = take(polymorphic :: (I32) -> I32)

{-
   A position whose **parameter** is not decided - Analysis-IR.md §11.4's G7c, from the other side.

   The rule that waypoint states is one-directional: a function position may be handed over with its
   **result** still open, because what fills it is what decides it, and never with an open parameter.
   A lambda writes no parameter types at all and takes them from the position, so an open one is a
   value nothing could make - and a named function has no arity to be looked up at either, which is
   the message that comes out.

   `Outcome.yana` is the other half, where an open result is handed over and answered.
-}
fn (p) supplied(body: (p) -> I32) -> I32 = 0

fn openParameter() -> I32 = supplied(twice)
